WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.140
Wolves entering 29 Wisconsin counties cut dear vehicle collisions by about 24%. This is unintended consequences, episode 149. The path runs from the recovery that brought the Wolves back through the roads the animals started using to the policy debates that followed, and then the lesson. In the winter of 2009, a radio-colored wolf crossed a county road in northern Wisconsin. The wolf paused and sent marked a stretch of pavement that also served as a dear travel corridor.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.340
store. Within two years, local deer began shifting their movement away from that same stretch.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.820
The change did not show up first as a howl in the woods. It showed up in the county sheriff's

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.620
thinner stack of collision reports. Jennifer Rainer was a graduate student when she started

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.480
studying wolves. State and federal agencies had spent decades trying to bring the animals

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:55.040
back under the Endangered Species Act. The goal was straightforward. Restore an apex predator

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.820
whose removal had let white-tailed deer numbers climb unchecked across the great lake states.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.920
Managers and biologists saw recovery as an ecological correction.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.580
They also hoped that correction would ease chronic deer damage to farms and forests.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.920
At the time, the most visible costs of wolves were livestock losses.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.100
Those losses were easy to tally, and the state could write a check for them.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.640
Any wider benefit states speculative.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.640
Reiner later recalled that the costs of predators are salient and simple to measure.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.060
The benefits, she recalled, often run through indirect ecological pathways that are harder to put a number on.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.540
That is a fair description of the information managers had.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:41.560
A dead calf is a fact you can photograph. A deer that never steps onto a road at dusk is a fact you may never see.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.500
Wisconsin's wolves had grown from a handful of animals recolonizing from Minnesota and Michigan in the 1970s.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.060
By the early 2000s, the question was no longer whether wolves could persist.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.500
The question was how their presence would ripple outward into human systems.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:03.500
Endangered Species Act listings and the delisting attempts that followed, focused almost entirely

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.500
on pack counts and livestock compensation formulas.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.980
Road safety effects sat outside that formal accounting.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.900
Nobody had to be careless for that gap to open.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.780
The ledgers were built around the harms people already knew how to count.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:23.075
Grey wolves recolonize Wisconsin on their own, moving in from Minnesota and Michigan starting

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:28.755
in the mid-1970s, growth steadied after federal protection strengthened in the 1990s.

00:02:29.175 --> 00:02:34.595
By 2010, wolves occupied 29 of the 63 counties tracked in long-term records.

00:02:35.115 --> 00:02:39.395
The state briefly delisted wolves in 2012 and allowed limited hunting.

00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:42.795
Legal challenges brought the animals back onto the list.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:48.135
Through all of that, wildlife agencies kept their eyes on packed territories and on verified

00:02:48.135 --> 00:02:49.235
livestock claims.

00:02:50.055 --> 00:02:52.915
Effects on roads were not what the monitoring was built to catch.

00:02:53.555 --> 00:02:58.055
Department of Natural Resources maps recorded the year each county first held a resident

00:02:58.055 --> 00:02:58.395
pack.

00:02:58.975 --> 00:03:01.335
That record later became a natural experiment.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:06.335
Researchers could line the first pack year up against collision statistics and ask what

00:03:06.335 --> 00:03:07.915
changed after wolves arrived.

00:03:07.915 --> 00:03:12.575
early monitoring emphasized minimum wolf counts and compensation payments.

00:03:13.155 --> 00:03:17.715
Those payments averaged roughly $3 million statewide over 35 years.

00:03:18.195 --> 00:03:22.195
No one at the time collected systematic before and after data on deer movement

00:03:22.195 --> 00:03:25.515
along the paved roads wolves began using as travel corridors.

00:03:26.235 --> 00:03:29.095
The animals were being counted. The pavement was not.

00:03:30.245 --> 00:03:33.285
Rainer later worked with Corbett Granger and Dominic Parker.

00:03:33.285 --> 00:03:40.345
The team matched Wolf maps to deer vehicle collision data from 1988 to 2010, across those

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:41.385
63 counties.

00:03:41.985 --> 00:03:44.245
In the counties Wolves entered, collisions fell.

00:03:44.885 --> 00:03:48.345
Only about one quarter of that dropped trace to fewer deer overall.

00:03:49.005 --> 00:03:53.265
The larger share came from deer changing their behavior near roads that Wolves used as

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:54.005
travel corridors.

00:03:54.605 --> 00:03:59.765
The study was published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, PNAS in

00:03:59.765 --> 00:04:00.725
2021.

00:04:00.725 --> 00:04:07.885
one. The average county saved roughly $375,000 a year in collision costs. Across Wisconsin,

00:04:08.025 --> 00:04:13.985
the annual total reached about $10.9 million. That figure was roughly 63 times what the

00:04:13.985 --> 00:04:18.625
state paid in a typical year to compensate farmers for verified wolf kills of livestock.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:24.065
Picture the comparison this way. For every dollar the compensation program paid out in

00:04:24.065 --> 00:04:28.825
a year, the roads were quietly returning many times that amount in crashes that did not

00:04:28.825 --> 00:04:34.085
happen. Drivers on those roads experienced fewer nighttime impacts. Speed limits

00:04:34.085 --> 00:04:39.565
did not change. Fencing did not change. Traffic volume did not change. The mechanism

00:04:39.565 --> 00:04:44.685
sat in how wolves move. Wolves treat roads as efficient hunting paths. They

00:04:44.685 --> 00:04:49.665
travel those paths at dusk and dawn. The same hours when deer are active. The

00:04:49.665 --> 00:04:54.005
result is what researchers call a landscape of fear. Dears stay farther from

00:04:54.005 --> 00:04:58.745
the pavement edge even when overall deer density falls only modestly. Of the

00:04:58.745 --> 00:05:02.205
The reduction, roughly 6 percentage points, came from lower deer numbers.

00:05:02.845 --> 00:05:05.825
The remaining 18 points came from behavioral avoidance.

00:05:06.365 --> 00:05:10.885
The researchers isolated that split by comparing counties with similar deer harvest rates

00:05:10.885 --> 00:05:12.465
but different wolf occupancy.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.105
The split matters for a practical reason.

00:05:15.805 --> 00:05:17.525
Recreational hunters removed deer.

00:05:18.125 --> 00:05:23.145
Hunters do not patrol the same road corridors at the same times, or with the same consistency

00:05:23.145 --> 00:05:24.105
that wolves do.

00:05:24.105 --> 00:05:28.045
Hunter activity does not generate the same localized avoidance.

00:05:28.585 --> 00:05:32.925
A deer can learn that a particular stretch of pavement is a bad place to linger at dusk.

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:38.885
A deer does not learn that lesson from a hunting season that happens somewhere else in the county, on a different clock.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:46.285
The data ran from 1988 to 2010. Wolfpacks established territories gradually across those years.

00:05:46.805 --> 00:05:51.145
The team could treat each county's first documented pack year as the start of the effect.

00:05:51.145 --> 00:05:55.725
No corresponding drop appeared in the 34 counties that remained wolf-free.

00:05:56.165 --> 00:06:00.105
The pattern held after the researchers controlled for changes in traffic volume

00:06:00.105 --> 00:06:01.825
and in reporting practices.

00:06:02.365 --> 00:06:07.005
So the $10.9 million is not a guess about deer disappearing from the landscape.

00:06:07.545 --> 00:06:10.185
It reflects fewer crashes and the medical costs,

00:06:10.645 --> 00:06:14.865
vehicle repairs, and insurance costs that each avoided incident did not generate.

00:06:15.385 --> 00:06:18.845
That is a second order effect sitting on top of a first order one.

00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:24.505
Wolves changed where deer stood, where deer stood changed who hit them, who was not hit

00:06:24.505 --> 00:06:27.725
changed what counties spent on wrecks, injuries and claims.

00:06:28.325 --> 00:06:31.325
None of that required a new speed limit or a new mile offense.

00:06:32.573 --> 00:06:37.713
Wildlife managers in Wisconsin and in neighboring states began citing the collision data when

00:06:37.713 --> 00:06:40.633
they weighed wolf hunting seasons and delisting petitions.

00:06:41.093 --> 00:06:43.713
The finding did not reverse any policy outright.

00:06:44.253 --> 00:06:47.873
It did supply a concrete dollar figure that earlier debates had been missing.

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:54.653
Rainer noted that recreational hunting removes deer, yet does not recreate the same landscape of fear along roads.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:58.233
Hunters do not patrol the linear corridor's wolves' favor.

00:06:58.913 --> 00:07:03.933
Similar analyses are now underway in other states where wolf range overlaps dense road networks.

00:07:04.633 --> 00:07:09.893
The PNAS paper itself became part of public comment records during later delisting discussions.

00:07:10.613 --> 00:07:15.213
Agencies had a quantified co-benefit to set beside livestock compensation totals.

00:07:15.213 --> 00:07:18.413
No fencing program was altered as a direct result.

00:07:18.953 --> 00:07:22.053
No signage program was altered as a direct result, either.

00:07:22.593 --> 00:07:24.553
What shifted was the frame of the argument.

00:07:25.153 --> 00:07:27.673
The debate had lived inside ecology and agriculture.

00:07:28.373 --> 00:07:31.693
It now had to make room for measurable returns on road safety.

00:07:32.293 --> 00:07:35.573
The original managers had not been wrong to compensate ranchers.

00:07:35.973 --> 00:07:39.853
Those losses were real, and they were the cost the system knew how to see.

00:07:40.413 --> 00:07:42.253
The study did not erase that ledger.

00:07:42.253 --> 00:07:45.873
It added a column the listings and delistings had left blank.

00:07:46.861 --> 00:07:50.621
When Rayner's team compared counties that gained wolves with counties that did not,

00:07:51.101 --> 00:07:54.841
the largest safety gain showed up where wolves used roads as hunting paths.

00:07:55.421 --> 00:07:58.261
It did not show up simply where total deer numbers fell.

00:07:58.841 --> 00:08:00.901
That pattern is the mechanism worth keeping.

00:08:01.541 --> 00:08:06.781
A single management choice, maintaining wolf presence in a county, can move prey at specific

00:08:06.781 --> 00:08:09.181
places without a separate road safety budget.

00:08:09.181 --> 00:08:14.601
The same data set showed that the benefits scaled with wolf occupancy, not with hunter

00:08:14.601 --> 00:08:15.361
harvest rates.

00:08:15.941 --> 00:08:20.381
Managers cannot assume that human hunting stands in for every effect a predator produces.

00:08:21.121 --> 00:08:25.401
States now weighing wolf policy can put their own collision numbers and their own livestock

00:08:25.401 --> 00:08:30.641
compensation numbers into the Wisconsin framework and ask whether the same ratio holds.

00:08:31.181 --> 00:08:35.761
The study is a template for indirect benefits that appear only after a predator has changed

00:08:35.761 --> 00:08:38.501
find scale prey behavior along human infrastructure.

00:08:39.221 --> 00:08:44.221
If you follow how agencies count what a recovered animal is worth, environmental intelligence

00:08:44.221 --> 00:08:48.001
is the narrow show that stays with those ledgers after the headline fades.

00:08:48.521 --> 00:08:53.061
When your state next weighs a wolf season or a delisting, what would you watch for in

00:08:53.061 --> 00:08:53.581
the spreadsheet?

00:08:54.181 --> 00:08:57.921
Only the livestock checks or the dusk roads where the fear actually lives.

00:08:58.541 --> 00:08:59.761
That's the case for today.

00:09:00.321 --> 00:09:04.361
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone

00:09:04.361 --> 00:09:07.321
who'd find it useful and subscribe wherever you're listening.

00:09:07.821 --> 00:09:08.321
See you tomorrow.

